1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,200
BrowserLens: interactive diagnosis of web-agent trajectories
with a layered state-action graph. By Junyeong Song, Mingyu Kim,
and Seongkook Heo, UNIST. UIST 2026 Posters.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:12,000
A completed web-agent run leaves only a linear log;
loops, dead ends, and stalls hide across distant lines.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:16,200
The same run, viewed as a graph, shows the loop at a glance.
The following demo walks through one real failed run.

4
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,400
A web agent was asked to find French startups with open jobs
in the 2022 and 2023 Y Combinator batches. Did the run succeed?

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:33,100
The timeline preserves every step, but structure is hard to see:
near-identical navigations repeat, line after line.

6
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:40,400
At the end of the log the run has failed: it hit its step budget
while ping-ponging between two company pages.

7
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:48,600
The failed planner subgoal is marked in red. Easy to spot here,
easy to lose in a hundred log lines.

8
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:52,100
The Graph view reorganizes the same run around browser-state identity.
In the URL layer, revisits merge into a single node.

9
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:59,400
Visit count drives node size and color: this company page was
visited 35 times. Badges count the cues collapsed underneath.

10
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:04,500
Loop edges surface at the top level: red cycles trace
the run's ping-pong between pages.

11
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:11,300
Double-clicking drills down: the page opens into
the screens seen on it.

12
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:17,500
One level further, a screen expands into its state-anchored actions.

13
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:23,100
The action layer exposes a stall: a click that loops on itself,
ringed in red as a no-op.

14
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:28,300
Cues are attention signals, not failure labels.
Selecting the node opens its evidence.

15
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:33,000
The no-op check reports DOM and ARIA unchanged,
screenshot difference zero.

16
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:38,900
Before-and-after screenshots, the action replay, and the model's
own reasoning let the reviewer confirm or reject the hypothesis.

17
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:44,100
Here the evidence confirms the click changed nothing:
a stall, not progress.

18
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:49,600
Run statistics summarize the whole picture:
98 tool calls, 80 repeated calls, 13 cue hits.

19
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:56,100
BrowserLens: a browser-state-anchored graph and
a cue-to-evidence flow for diagnosing web-agent runs.

